blocks.explanation:
Lead has a high density and high atomic number, so it absorbs gamma rays strongly. Lead collimators (shields and thin septa) placed around and between the detectors:
1. allow only gamma rays coming from the patient along the proper directions to reach the detectors;
2. absorb scattered photons and gamma rays from outside the field of view (e.g. from other parts of the body);
3. so they reduce false (random and scattered) coincidences, improve image contrast and sharpness, and also shield the detectors.
